<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Leabua Jonathan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1914">1914</a>–<a href="/years/1987">1987</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Leabua Jonathan was the first prime minister of Lesotho, leading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-national-party">Basotho National Party</a> from independence in 1966. When he was losing the 1970 election he annulled it, suspended the constitution and ruled by decree, sidelining King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a>. His later quarrels with apartheid <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> led to a border <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and he was overthrown in a military coup in January 1986.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/leabua-jonathan.jpg" alt="Leabua Jonathan" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joost Evers / Anefo · CC BY-SA 3.0 nl · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Minister-president_Jonathan_van_Lesotho_(1970).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Leabua Jonathan was born on 30 October 1914 in Leribe. He was a minor chief and, like many others, a great-grandson of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-i">Moshoeshoe I</a>. He worked as a mine induna at Brakpan, but because he was a chief he returned home and from 1937 took part in local government in Basutoland. He was a member of the delegations to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> that sought self-government for the territory.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Jonathan converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a> and in 1959 founded the Basutoland National Party, which had the backing of Canadian Catholic missionaries and was renamed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-national-party">Basotho National Party</a> at independence. It came fourth in the 1960 election. In the elections of April 1965 it won 31 of the 60 parliamentary seats, although Jonathan lost his own seat and had to stand again later in a safe one. He took office as prime minister on 7 July 1965, succeeding Chief Sekhonyana Nehemia Maseribane, and after independence executive power passed to him from the British High Commissioner.</p><p>Because Lesotho is entirely surrounded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> and the majority of its people worked in the mines there, his government at first followed a policy of dialogue with its neighbour, and he believed he could deal with Verwoerd and Vorster. After the 1970 election was annulled, an appointed Interim National Assembly with an overwhelming pro-government majority was set up in 1973. Jonathan was hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pan-africanist-congress-of-azania">Pan Africanist Congress</a>, which backed the opposition <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basotho-congress-party">Basutoland Congress Party</a>, and drew closer to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> after the Lesotho Liberation Army, the exiled military wing of the Basutoland Congress Party, prepared to strike at Lesotho. In the late 1970s he accused South Africa of supporting that army, whose main force was wiped out in 1979.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Lesotho's support for the cross-border operations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/african-national-congress">African National Congress</a> led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> to all but close the country's land borders and to threaten more direct action. This pressure, together with discontent at home, ended in the coup of 20 January 1986, led by Major General Justin Metsing Lekhanya. A military government chaired by Lekhanya then ruled with King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moshoeshoe-ii">Moshoeshoe II</a>. Jonathan was placed under house arrest in August 1986 and died of a heart attack on 5 April 1987, at the age of 73. He received the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dag-hammarskjold">Dag Hammarskjöld</a> award in 1983, and in 2007 was made a Companion of O. R. Tambo (gold) after his death.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Leabua Jonathan” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Basutoland became independent on 4 October 1966 as the Kingdom of Lesotho, a constitutional monarchy within the Commonwealth with Moshoeshoe II, a descendant of Moshoeshoe I of the Basotho Kingdom, as king and <b>Leabua Jonathan</b> of the Basotho National Party as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-lesotho">Kingdom of Lesotho</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Hlotse, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pretoria">Pretoria</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/south-africa">South Africa</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-28.87,28.04&amp;year=1961" aria-label="Hlotse on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5282 3220 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Leabua Jonathan"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5732 3490h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5732 3490h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-28.87,28.04&amp;year=1961">What was here in 1961? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
